  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nIf I showed you the current water vapor satellite imagery and asked \nyou what date you thought this was from, I would not blame you if \nyou said November. There is a nice baroclinic leaf across the upper \nMS valley into the western Great Lakes and a dry slot moving across \nnortheast KS into southeast MN. Although today has been a washout \nand a rather crummy weather day, we haven't heard too many \ncomplaints coming out of a 2 week stretch where most places saw less \nthan a quarter of an inch of rain with highs frequently topping out \nin the 90s. We've seen some impressive rainfall totals today and \nhave seen the gauges to backup the MRMS rainfall estimate bullseye \nof 6-8\" of rain down around St. Peter and Le Sueur. Despite some \nrather larger rainfall numbers, this rain has come over a period of \nof 12 hours, not in just 3 or 4 hours, which means impacts from this \nrainfall (in terms of flooding) have been fairly minimal. Even \nthough this heavy rainfall fell almost entirely within the MN River \nBasin, rises along the river are expected to remain within the \nstream banks. No matter how you slice it, this was an incredibly \nbeneficial rain that has come with very few negative impacts.\n\nFor the weekend, we will not accept any complaints about the \nweather! We'll have rising heights and subsidence, which means \nmostly sunny skies with temepratures near normal and dewpoints \nretreating into the 50s and 60s. There has been some concern about \nsmoky skies returning, but visible satellite imagery this afternoon \nlooks pretty clean from the Dakotas back across Wyoming and Montana, \nso even chances for elevated smoke look pretty limited this weekend \nas well. Get out there and enjoy the outdoors this weekend, we're \nonly 54 days away from the earliest snowfall on record here in the \nTwin Cities! (September 24, 1985)\n\nWe'll start next week with a cold front dropping across the area \nMonday night through Tuesday morning. Right now, we look to miss-\ntime this front. We'll be getting the leftovers of whatever happens \nin the Dakotas and northwest MN Monday afternoon late Monday night \nin central/western MN, while the front should be far enough along \nTuesday afternoon to where we'll be too far west to see any storms \nthat form with daytime heating on Tuesday. Behind this front, we'll \nget another hit of pleasant Canadian air for the middle of next week.",
